# Line endings are a portability contract, not a preference.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (2026-08-09)
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` is Git for Windows' default, so a Windows checkout gets CRLF. That is
# harmless for a file Windows reads -- and fatal for a file Linux EXECUTES, because the kernel reads
# the shebang literally:
#
#     #!/usr/bin/env bash\r   ->   /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#
# That is not hypothetical. `scripts/quality/firstrun-harness` -- the harness whose entire job is to
# prove NPDev's instructions work on a machine that starts with nothing -- could not start at all
# from this checkout. `COPY run-readme.sh` baked the CRLF shebang into the image, and the container
# died on its first line.
#
# **The tool that exists to catch "works on my machine" was itself not portable**, and it failed only
# on Windows, so CI -- which checks out LF on a Linux runner -- stayed green the whole time. Seven
# earlier defects in this repo share that shape; this is the eighth.
#
# `scripts/quality/check-executable-line-endings.py` enforces the general rule (any file declaring a
# shebang must be LF) because THIS FILE IS A DECLARATION AND DECLARATIONS DRIFT: a new extension, a
# generator, or an editor can reintroduce CRLF that no glob here covers.

# Sensible default: let git decide what is text, and normalise it in the repository.
* text=auto

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EXECUTED ON LINUX -- CRLF makes these unrunnable, not merely untidy.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf
gradlew         text eol=lf
npdev           text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
Dockerfile.*    text eol=lf
*.dockerfile    text eol=lf

# Python: conventionally LF, and several of these carry a shebang and are COPY'd into containers.
*.py            text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WINDOWS-NATIVE -- CRLF is correct here. PowerShell in particular is happiest with it, and these
# are never executed by a Linux kernel.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.ps1           text eol=crlf
*.psm1          text eol=crlf
*.psd1          text eol=crlf
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf
gradlew.bat     text eol=crlf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BINARY -- never touch these. A "helpful" line-ending conversion corrupts them silently, which is
# the worst failure mode available: the file still exists and is no longer valid.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.pdf           binary
*.zip           binary
*.jar           binary
*.exe           binary
*.dll           binary
*.so            binary
*.dylib         binary
*.AppImage      binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
*.ttf           binary
